Ah — yes, this is the classic **block-scoped `const` swallowed inside a `try`** issue, and Babel is doing exactly what we told it to, but not what we *meant*.

You declared `const savedForm` inside the `try`, so it's scoped to that block only. When Babel transpiles the code, it correctly moves the outer logic forward — but `savedForm` is undefined outside that block.

---

### 🔥 The Bug:
```js
try {
  const fileContent = await this.fileSystem.readFile(data.path);
  const savedForm = JSON.parse(fileContent);
} catch (err) {
  ...
}
// 🔴 savedForm is undefined here
await this.ui.dashboard.restoreFromSavedForm(savedForm);
```

---

### ✅ The Fix:
Lift `savedForm` out of the `try` block by declaring it earlier:

```js
'galyleo:load': async (data) => {
  this.dashboardFilePath = data.path;
  let savedForm = null;

  try {
    const fileContent = await this.fileSystem.readFile(data.path);
    savedForm = JSON.parse(fileContent);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(`Error loading or parsing dashboard file "${data.path}":`, err);
    return;
  }

  try {
    this.lastData = { savedForm };
    await this.ui.dashboard.restoreFromSavedForm(savedForm);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Error restoring dashboard from saved form:', err);
    return;
  }

  await this.view.whenRendered();

  const loadScreen = document.getElementById('loading-screen');
  if (loadScreen) loadScreen.remove();

  this.relayout();
},
```

---

### 💡 Why It Matters:
In Babel-generated ES5, `const`/`let` become `var`, and `var` hoisting plus async flow can lead to variables being “declared” but not defined at the right time.

By lifting `savedForm`, we ensure it’s in the right lexical scope for all branches of the logic — and Babel won’t get tripped up trying to be helpful.

